In order to qualitatively determine the applicability of TAE and FAE in simulating the HHG spectra, we introduce the concept of error rate expressed as: 
\begin{equation}
	\delta =\frac{\left\lvert P_{j}^{(TAE/FAE)}(\Omega_c)-P_{j}^{(NSFA)}(\Omega_c) \right\rvert }{P_{j}^{(NSFA)}(\Omega_c)}. 
	\label{eq:error rate}
\end{equation}
In the equation above, the error rate $\delta$ of HHG intensity is a relative error. It is defined as the ratio of the difference between NSFA and TAE/FAE results over NSFA results. For the region where the energy is greater than the HHG cutoff energy, the calculation of NSFA for the S1 trajectory will diverge unphysically {\cite{Auguste_2012}}, so the photon energy $\Omega_c$ chosen here is slightly lower than the cutoff energy to avoid numerical divergence. 

