\subsection{Omni-router learns specialized experts}
To investigate whether the sharing of router decisions can lead to more specialized experts, we did a detailed analysis of the expert usage per token in the transformer-based ASR model with MoE layers, see Fig.~\ref{fig:expert_usage}. We visualize three randomly selected audio samples from the Librispeech \textit{dev-other} dataset. Each example includes (from top to bottom): the mel-spectrogram, the expert assignments for a 4-expert Switch Transformer, and the expert assignments for a 4-expert Omni-router Transformer. Expert assignments are depicted across time steps on the horizontal axis and transformer layers on the vertical axis. 
Note that there is no specific meaning of the colors of the experts, and for Switch Transformer plots, colors are permuted to show the optimal mapping across layers.

We observe notable differences between the routing behaviors of the Switch Transformer and the Omni-router Transformer. The Omni-router Transformer demonstrates a markedly structured and coherent pattern of expert usage across both time and layers, whereas the Switch Transformer exhibits a comparatively scattered and less structured routing pattern. Specifically, the Omni-router's routing decisions appear to form consistent temporal segments, suggesting meaningful coordination between layers. Moreover, there is a clear structure and usage of the same expert across the model's depth, showing that the inductive bias of omni-router holds and provides consistent specialization. Furthermore, the Omni-router's expert assignments clearly correlate with acoustic patterns in the mel-spectrogram. For instance, the green expert predominantly handles silent segments, whereas alternating stripes of orange, blue, and pink correspond to distinct speech regions. In contrast, the Switch Transformer displays more fragmented expert assignments, indicative of weaker inter-layer coordination and potentially less effective specialization. 

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{specialization.png}
  \caption{Relative WER change percentage of Switch Transformer ASR and Omni-router Transformer ASR at different permutation ratios $p$.}
  \label{fig:specialization}
\end{figure}

To further quantify expert specialization in both models, we adopt the expert permutation method introduced by prior work~\cite{dai2024deepseekmoe}. Specifically, for each token, instead of using the top-1 expert selected by the router, we randomly select an expert with a certain probability $p$ and evaluate the impact on performance by monitoring changes in WER on the Librispeech \textit{dev-other} set across varying permutation ratios. Our hypothesis is that the Omni-router Transformer ASR will exhibit a larger performance degradation (increased WER) than the Switch Transformer ASR, indicating greater expert specialization.

As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:specialization}, we observe that as the permutation ratio $p$ increases, the Omni-router Transformer exhibits a higher sensitivity to incorrect expert selection, resulting in a more pronounced degradation in performance compared to the Switch Transformer ASR. This finding supports our hypothesis of enhanced expert specialization in the Omni-router model.

