%	\section{Conclusion}\label{sec: conclusion}
%{\color{blue}	We consider the sparse nonlinear least square problems, where the Jacobian matrices, though unavailable or costly to compute, possess an underlying sparse structure. We construct the sparse Jacobian models by solving $ \ell_1 $ minimization problems subject to a small number of random interpolation constraints, and propose a LM method based on such models. It is shown that the sparse Jacobian models are first-order accurate under certain conditions. Furthermore, we prove that the sequence generated by the proposed method converges almost surely to a stationary point of the merit function. Numerical results validate the efficiency of the proposed method for derivative-free sparse nonlinear least square problems.
