Note that a similar approach for the 3/2 model with constant coefficients has been explored in \cite{DetempleKitapbayev2017}, albeit using the transition density function. Additionally, an infinite-series representation for the transition density of the time-inhomogeneous 3/2 model was derived in \cite{Meesa2025}, where all coefficients are expressed in terms of Bell polynomials and generalized Laguerre polynomials.


\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we use the change-of-variable formulae derived in \cite{Peskir2005,Peskir2007Surf} to prove generalized decomposition formula \cref{decompGenN} for pricing American options under various time-inhomogeneous models. These models include pure diffusion processes (including multidimensional diffusion such as stochastic volatility models, etc.), jump-diffusion models, and models with jumps of finite and infinite variation and activity.

Since for many such models the transition density is not known in closed form, but the CF is available, we propose representing the density via the CF using the representation provided by the COS method of \cite{FangOosterlee2008}. Once this representation is established, computing the option price requires two main steps: first, solving the integral Volterra equation (potentially multidimensional) of the second kind to find the EB, and then computing the European option price (usually via FFT) and the EEP using numerical quadratures.

Alternatively, the EB can be found using the GIT approach described in \cite{ItkinMuravey2024jd,Itkin2024jd}, which also results in solving integral Volterra equation(s) of the second kind, but often with a more explicit (and therefore more easily computed) kernel.

Solving integral equations is typically faster and more accurate compared to other methods for pricing American options, such as FD, LSMC, etc. See the discussion in \cite{ItkinLiptonMuraveyBook,Andersen2016} and references therein for further details.

Regarding the novelty of this approach, we carefully analyze the existing literature to demonstrate that a similar decomposition approach was previously developed only for two time-homogeneous models: the Heston model in \cite{Chiarella2005} and the Merton jump-diffusion model in \cite{Chiarella2009}. However, the COS method was never used for this purpose; instead, the density in these papers was computed using inverse FFT, which is computationally more challenging. Therefore, our framework provides both a significant generalization of the method and improved computational speed and accuracy. At the same time, as per \cite{AndersenNUFFT},  the COS method could be unstable for small maturities and in some other cases. Then, using the NUFFT method of \cite{AndersenNUFFT} could be preferable.



