\begin{abstract}

% Spin mixtures of ultracold fermions are a cornerstone of quantum many-body physics, enabling superfluidity, polaron formation, and rich spin dynamics through their tunable $s$-wave interactions. Introducing strong, long-range dipolar forces into such mixtures promises even more exotic phases, but has so far been hampered by instability and limited control. Microwave dressing can bind two polar molecules into a loosely held “field-linked” pair which is stabilized by repulsive interactions, shielding the gas from a dipolar attraction driven collapse. Here, by coupling a circularly polarized microwave field to a spin mixture of dipolar molecules, we are able to realize highly-tunable, $s$-wave interactions on top of the intrinsic long-range dipolar interactions. Remarkably, the $s$-wave interaction tunability is universally captured by simple expressions in terms of the microwave field parameters. This can be exploited to realize efficient evaporative cooling and opens new routes to stable, strongly interacting dipolar Fermi gases.

% % Another suggestion for the abstract (you can combine them also)
% Spin mixtures of ultracold fermions underpin much of quantum many-body physics, enabling superfluidity, polaron formation, and rich spin dynamics. Combining them with microwave-shielded dipolar molecules introduces field-linked resonances that create tunable $s$-wave interactions without sacrificing gas stability. These interactions obey universal scaling laws, letting us predict the resonance position, width, and bound-state energy across a broad span of microwave parameters and molecular species. Their large elastic-to-loss ratio, accessible microwave field parameters without the need for elliptic polarization, and absence of deeply bound states makes them extremely promising for evaporation, providing an efficient pathway to deeply degenerate dipolar Fermi gases.


Spin mixtures of degenerate fermions are a cornerstone of quantum many-body physics, enabling superfluidity, polarons, and rich spin dynamics through $s$-wave scattering resonances. Combining them with strong, long-range dipolar interactions provides highly flexible control schemes promising even more exotic quantum phases. Recently, microwave shielding gave access to spin-polarized degenerate samples of dipolar fermionic molecules, where tunable $p$-wave interactions were enabled by field-linked resonances available only by compromising the shielding. Here, we study the scattering properties of a fermionic dipolar spin mixture and show that a universal $s$-wave resonance is readily accessible without compromising the shielding.
We develop a universal description of the tunable $s$-wave interaction and weakly bound tetratomic states based on the microwave-field parameters.
The $s$-wave resonance paves the way to stable, controllable and strongly-interacting dipolar spin mixtures of deeply degenerate fermions and supports favorable conditions to reach this regime via evaporative cooling.
\end{abstract}

