By coupling the rotational ground state to the first excited manifold with a blue-detuned circularly or elliptically polarized microwave field, an avoided crossing forms in the inter-molecular potential landscape, resulting in a field-induced barrier that dramatically suppresses inelastic losses at short range \cite{Cooper2009,Deng:2023, Huang:2012,Karman2018,Lassabliere2018,Anderegg:2021}. This further enables the tuning of the scattering properties through coupling to field-linked bound states that emerge in the interaction potential for suitable field parameters \cite{Avdeenkov:2002,Avdeenkov:2003,Avdeenkov:2004,Gorshkov2008,Cooper2009,Huang:2012,Lassabliere2018,matsuda_resonant_2020, Chen:2023,Chen:2024}. Microwave shielding has recently led to the creation of a stable degenerate dipolar Fermi gas \cite{Schindewolf:2022}, and 
