\section*{The Assignment}

The goal is to see how researchers working in different disciplines share materials for replication of empirical statistical analyses (and what software they use). We will visualize the results once everyone has done their small piece of data collection. For your part, this means: (a) visiting a website relating to a published paper, (b) exploring it in a structured way, (c) putting what you find into an online survey, and (d) recording in \textcolor{blue}{[Course software]} that you have done so.

This will always begin with a ``URL'' or ``DOI stub.'' If it is a URL, you can follow it directly.  If it is a ``DOI stub'' (such as {\tt 123.456/abc/def}), you would go to the website {\ttfamily {\bfseries https://doi.org/}123.456/abc/def} which will lead to an online journal or data repository.  Steps after that vary, as described below.

\subsection*{Basic procedure for file types}
The main information we will be gathering is about the extension at the ends of filenames. This extension indicates the type of the file and, typically, the kind of software with which that file is used.   Occasionally there could be \textbf{other} file types or ambiguous or complex cases, and there will be a space for you to describe those. There are \textcolor{blue}{[N]} kinds of exploration you will do:

\begin{enumerate}
%\begin{itemize}
    \item (2x) \textbf{Warm-up}
    \item (\textcolor{blue}{N}x) \textbf{Political Science/Economics}: Harvard Dataverse
    \item (\textcolor{blue}{N}x) \textbf{Statistics}: 
    %Journal of Applied Statistics (JoAS) / \\
        Journal of the American Statistical Association (JASA) 
    \item \textcolor{blue}{Any other journals/sources}
%\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

When you find a ``replication package,'' ``code repository,'' or ``github page'' with data and/or analysis files, there are a few main file types to look out for:

\begin{itemize}
    \item R files (ending in {\tt .R}, {\tt .Rda}, and {\tt .Rmd})
    \item Stata files (ending in {\tt .do}, {\tt .ado}, and {\tt .dta})
    \item Python files (ending in {\tt .py})
    \item Matlab files (ending in {\tt .m})
    \item SAS files (ending in {\tt .sas} and {\tt .sas7bdat})
%    \item SAS data files (ending in )
    \item SPSS files (ending in {\tt .spss} and {\tt .sav})
%    \item SPSS data files (ending in )
    \item C/C++ files (ending in {\tt .c})
\end{itemize}

In each case, the question is not how many of each file there are, it is simply \textbf{whether} each type of file is ever present.

If you find a statistical package \textbf{besides} those listed (e.g. FORTRAN, Julia, etc), select either the option ``Other: ... '' or ``Ambiguous: ... '' after the main file type options and describe the package in the next text field.

One tricky step with replication packages and repository entries is that they might contain \textbf{compressed} folders; these compressed folders contain more files to be investigated.  Such compressed folders are often given {\tt .zip} or {\tt .z} or {\tt .gz} or {\tt .tar.gz} file extensions.  In those cases, you should open the compressed folder and take note of  each kind of statistical package file that you see  inside it. %, in addition to taking note of which kinds of statistical package files are \emph{not} inside it. %{\bfseries not} inside it.

If a journal article is not accompanied by a replication package, or at least you do not immediately find the code, then \textbf{skim} the article to see if it mentions any of these terms which might lead you to the files:
\underline{software},
\underline{scripts},
\underline{code},
\underline{version},
\underline{package},
\underline{github},
\underline{replication},
\underline{reproducing},
\underline{py}. 

\subsection*{Keep (rough) track of how long this takes in total}
When you are done, one of the two questions in the \textcolor{blue}{[Course software]} quiz will be how long the ten DOIs took you in total.  So keep (rough) track please!

\subsection*{If you encounter a very time-consuming case}
Occasionally a single DOI will lead to many hundreds of files, or be difficult in some other time-consuming  way.  Do your best, but please do \textbf{not} spend more than fifteen minutes on \textbf{any one} of the ten assigned DOIs.  If it takes more than 15 minutes, stop work on that DOI, proceed by moving on to other DOIs, and describe which DOI was problematic in the \textcolor{blue}{[Course software]} quiz when you are done. 

\subsection*{If anything else goes wrong}
If you make a mistake or find something unusually confusing about a particular entry, make a note of the DOI and/or the completion code you get at the end of the online survey, and mention either or both of them in your comments in the \textcolor{blue}{[Course software]} quiz when you are done.  This will help us resolve any issues later.

\subsection*{Where is the online survey?}
\textcolor{blue}{[URL HERE]}


\subsection*{How do I get started?}
Start with the warm-ups on the next page.


\section{Warm-up}
To familiarize yourself with the online survey, the first step is to do warm-up surveys.  There are \textbf{two pretend entries} in this category, described below.  All online surveys for this exercise will begin with a (pretend) ``DOI stub'' meaning a Digital Object Identifier.  Online surveys end by producing a completion code in case you need it for referencing any problem that came up.

\subsection{Warm-up 1}

In the first warm-up, fill out the online survey as though you are doing a Harvard Dataverse exploration. The DOI stub you should enter into the online survey is {\tt 123.456/STUB/1/HD} (you don't really have to go there on the web, as it won't work).  Imagine there you find a replication package with some R {\tt .R} files, some Stata {\tt .dta} and {\tt .do} files, a {\tt .zip} folder that in turn contains some Python {\tt .py} files, and otherwise only text and pdf documentation.

Once you go through the survey and answer the questions accordingly, you will see  a ``completion code''  at the end in case you need it for reference.

\subsection{Warm-up 2}

In the second warm-up, you should fill out the online survey as though you are exploring a JASA article.  The DOI stub you should enter in the survey is {\tt 234.567/STUB/2/JASA}.  Imagine you find that the article itself mentions using Python, saying (``we use Python package \emph{autobounds} and python solver \emph{cvxpy}''), but that even when you look at the Supplemental tab, you cannot find any replication files, just additional graphs and text. 
%find a repository with a single compressed {\tt .zip} folder. Imagine you opened that folder, finding that it contained three python {\tt .py} files, and otherwise only text and pdf documentation files.
As before, once you complete the survey, you will get a completion code in case you need it.


\section{Harvard Dataverse}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{[N]}} of the DOI stubs assigned to you (in a list or spreadsheet provided by your professor) will be   Harvard Dataverse entries. For these entries, you will be given a DOI stub. If you enter that stub (say, ``{\tt 123.456/7}'') into a web browser after typing {\tt https://doi.org/} (so, altogether, typing something like {\tt https://doi.org/123.456/7}), the browser will take you to a repository entry with replication files in it. Browse or download those files, noting what kinds of files you see, as described in the \textbf{Basic procedure for file types} above.  


%As always,
%Save the completion code at the end for entry into \textcolor{blue}{[Course software]}.


\section{JASA}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{[N]}} of the DOI stubs assigned to you (in a list or spreadsheet provided by your professor) will %You will be assigned \textbf{four}
lead to a journal article in JASA.  (Put {\tt https://doi.org/} in front of the stub, as before.) In order to access JASA articles it is easiest to go through the campus network but if you are off-campus you should be able to access articles through the library. It may or may not have replication files associated with it.  At the top of the article there are typically tabs for ``Full Article,'' ``Figures \& data,'' ``References,'' and ``Supplemental;'' it is under the ``\textbf{Supplemental}'' tab that one can often find a .zip file with code. If there is such a .zip file, repository, or replication package, explore it following the usual steps in the \textbf{Basic procedure for file types}.

%\section{JASA and JoAS}
%You will be assigned \textbf{four} entries in this category: two JASA entries each, and two JoAS entries each. For these entries, you will have a DOI that takes you directly to a journal article.  It may or may not have a replication package or file repository associated with it; such a package may be mentioned in the article or linked from supplementary materials at the end of the article.  Sometimes at the top of the article there are tabs for ``Full Article,'' ``Figures \& data,'' ``References,'' and ``Supplemental,'' and it is often by following the ``Supplemental'' link that one can find a .zip file with a package of code files. If there is such a .zip file, repository, or replication package, follow the usual steps for exploring it.

Occasionally, a journal article will not be directly accompanied by such files. In those cases you should skim the article for key words which might lead you to either the files or a description of the files:
\underline{software},
\underline{scripts},
\underline{code},
\underline{version},
\underline{package},
\underline{github},
\underline{replication},
\underline{reproducing},
\underline{py}. 
In some cases, these keywords may lead you to discover that replication files are linked from within the article itself.  If you find this link, follow it and characterize the files you find there.  If you do not find this, but you find a description of the software without any link to the actual code, note what software is described, and note the text of the sentence(s) in the article indicating what software was used. 
%Fill out the survey accordingly.  If some other statistical software is mentioned, you can describe it in the survey.

