It is instructive to explicitely write common cross-power spectra. 
For instance the galaxy - CMB lensing power spectrum are written as:
%
\begin{equation}
C_\ell^{gg} = \int d\chi \frac{W_g^2(z)}{\chi^2} P_{gg} \left(k = \frac{\ell + \frac{1}{2}}{\chi}, z\right) \; ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
C_\ell^{\kappa g} = \int d\chi \frac{W_g(z) W_\kappa(z)}{\chi^2} P_{gm} \left(k = \frac{\ell + \frac{1}{2}}{\chi}, z\right) \; ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
C_\ell^{\kappa\kappa} = \int d\chi \frac{W_\kappa^2(z)}{\chi^2} P_{mm} \left(k = \frac{\ell + \frac{1}{2}}{\chi}, z\right) \; ,
\end{equation}
%
where $\chi$ is the comoving distance to redshift $z$ along the line of sight, $P_{mm}$, $P_{gg}$, and $P_{gm}$ are the three dimensional power spectra of the matter, galaxy clustering and their cross-correlation, respectively. $W_g$ and $W_\kappa$ are the galaxy and CMB lensing kernels defined by:
%
\begin{equation}
W_g(z) = H(z) \frac{dN}{dz} \; ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
W_\kappa(z) = \frac{3}{2} \Omega_m H_0^2 (1 + z) \frac{\chi (\chi_{\star} - \chi)}{\chi_{\star}} \; .
\end{equation}
%
Here, $dN/dz$ is the normalized redshift distribution of the galaxy sample, and $\chi_{\star}$ is the comoving distance to the surface of last scattering.  

