\begin{abstract}
We consider the problem of implementing deontic modal logic. We show how (deontic) modal operators can be expressed elegantly using default negation (negation-as-failure) and strong negation present in answer set programming (ASP). We propose using global constraints of ASP to represent obligations and impermissibilities of deontic modal logic. We show that our proposed representation results in the various paradoxes of deontic modal logic being elegantly resolved. 
\keywords{Modal Logic, Deontic Logic, Logic Programming, Answer Set Programming}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
% ART: We focus on deontic logic and modal logics in general during the introduction, but wouldn't it be better to just say deontic and alethic logics, which are both forms of modal logic? 
%GG: That's how they are traditionally presented (e.g., Stanford encyclopedia); It seems the term Alethic Logic is not used, rather Alethic Modal Logic (but Deontic logic is used without the word Modal). Lot of this is due to historical reasons.
Modal logics extend classical logic by introducing modal operators like ``it is necessary that'' and ``it is possible that'' to reason about possibility, necessity, and related modalities. It is used to formally model statements about what could, must, or ought to be true across possible worlds or circumstances.
Deontic modal logic is a modal logic that formalizes reasoning about normative concepts such as obligation (\textrm{OB}), permission (\textrm{PE}), and impermissibility (\textrm{IM}). It uses modal operators to express statements like ``It ought to be the case that A'' or ``It is permitted that B''. Deontic logic helps model ethical, legal, and normative systems by capturing how actions are classified as obligatory, permissible, or forbidden.

Modal logics, including deontic logic, have been heavily studied for decades. In this paper, we focus on showing how modal logics can be represented in answer set programming (ASP), a formalism for knowledge representation and reasoning \cite{cacm-asp,gelfondkahl}. Our work is motivated by our quest to develop the s(CASP) system---a goal-driven predicate answer set programming system---and to show that it can be used to emulate and automate a large part of human thinking \cite{scasp,gupta-csr}. The negation operator used in modal logics can be divided into two categories: negation appearing next to a proposition and that appearing next to a modal operator. The former is mapped to  \textit{strong} negation of ASP and the latter is mapped to \textit{default} negation (also called negation-as-failure). We show that this mapping is compatible with the \textit{traditional threefold classification} and the \textit{modal square} (and \textit{deontic square}) \textit{of opposition} \cite{seph-deontic-logic}. We discuss how modal logics can be represented in ASP. In particular, we propose using ASP's global constraints for representing obligations and impermissibilities. This results in quite an elegant encoding of deontic logic formulas in ASP. We show that the paradoxes of deontic logic are elegantly resolved by using this representation. Finally, we give a larger example to illustrate our ideas.

The main contribution of this paper is to show how modal logics, particularly deontic logic, 
% ART: Phrasing suggestion: "how modal logics, with special care for deontic logic, can be directly..."
can be directly represented in ASP in an elegant manner, and how this representation elegantly allows the paradoxes of deontic logics to be resolved. Our work is a step towards showing that goal-directed predicate ASP indeed is a good candidate for knowledge representation and reasoning and for emulating parts of human thinking. Much of the insight for this work comes from our attempt to use the s(CASP) goal-directed predicate ASP system for modeling modal logics \cite{CODORD}.  

\section{Modeling Human Thinking with ASP} 
% ART: Suggested rephrasing: "Modeling Human Thought with ASP"
%GG: Human Thinking is the common term that is used (e.g., title of Kowalski's book)
A large part of human thinking can be simulated with three types of ASP rules: default rules with exceptions and preferences (defeasible rules), integrity constraints (denials), and assumption-based (or abductive) reasoning~\cite{gupta-csr,gelfondkahl,comp-thinking}. 
%
%These three types of rules cover all of predicate-based ASP, a Turing-complete language,
%\comm{I think ASP is not Turing-complete. A paper from Cabalar about this topic: \url{https://www.dc.fi.udc.es/~cabalar/mg65.pdf}}, 
%GG: good point. Propositional ASP is not turing complete, but s(CASP) is a Turing complete language, complete with an operational semantics. Thanks to pointing to Cabalar's paper; it's an interesting read. 
%so one can arguably draw the conclusion that a large part of human thinking can be modeled with just these three constructs.
% ART: Passive voice here, suggested rephrasing: 
These three constructs are also the principal components of (predicate-based) ASP, a Turing-complete language.\footnote{Propositional ASP is not Turing complete; however, the predicate-based ASP that s(CASP) implements (and provides an operational semantics for) is indeed Turing complete, since it is a superset of Prolog.} Arguably, then predicate ASP is a good candidate for faithfully modeling (a large subset of) human thinking.

\smallskip\noindent{\bf Default Rules:} 
%Given a goal, we prove it by decomposing it into subgoals, and then recursively proving those subgoals next, and so on. 
% ART: Suggested rephrasing: "
A given goal is proven by decomposing it into subgoals, each of which are recursively proved following a similar process. 
The dependence of goals on subgoals is described by logic programming rules. For example:

\begin{lstlisting}
flies(X) :- bird(X), alive(X).
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent However, there might be conditions that might prevent us from drawing the conclusion in the head of the rule. These conditions represent exceptions. Thus, the above rule may be refined into: 

\begin{lstlisting}
flies(X) :- bird(X), alive(X), not exception(X).
exception(X) :- penguin(X).
\end{lstlisting}


\noindent where if \code{X} is a penguin it will prevent \code{flies(X)} from succeeding. There may be multiple rules 
% ART: Multiple rules, or multiple exceptions specifically? This section is not very clear. Why is arranging the rules in priority order relevant?
%GG: A concept may be defined via multiple default rules, and so we prioritize them (Ch 5 of Gelfond's book).
used to reduce a goal. We can add additional subgoals to the rules that will allow us to discriminate among these rules allowing us to arrange these rules in some kind of priority order~\cite{gelfondkahl}. 

\smallskip\noindent{\bf Assumption-based Reasoning}:
%
While reducing a goal \code{g}, if no information is available for it, i.e., no rule is defined for \code{g}, we can perform {\it abductive} or {\it assumption-based} reasoning. That is, we now consider two alternate worlds, one in which \code{g} is true and another one in which \code{g} is false. This is achieved by adding the following rules (termed \textit{even loop over negation} in ASP literature~\cite{cacm-asp}). 

\begin{lstlisting}
g :- not not_g.
not_g :- not g.
\end{lstlisting}


\noindent In the s(CASP) system, the above effect is achieved by simply declaring \code{g} as an abducible via the directive:

\begin{lstlisting}
#abducible g.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent 
%An abducible can be an arbitrary predicate as well.
% ART: Suggested rephrasing (if I understand it correctly): "
Such an abducible can be asserted for any arbitrary predicate. 
% ART: It might be good to re-emphasize here that Prolog/regular ASP can't do this
%GG: actually regular ASP should be able to do it since it has even loops over negation. Prolog does not have many of the things that ASP has (inlcuding contraints) so we can assume that the readers already know this.
The directive automatically generates the even loop over negation described above.

\smallskip\noindent{\bf Global Integrity Constraints (Denials):} As we accumulate subgoals in the reduction process, we may encounter subgoals that are mutually inconsistent. These subgoals may occur  far apart in the goal reduction process, and their inconsistency cannot be easily modeled using the exception mechanism described previously. We can check for such  inconsistencies via integrity constraints. We term such integrity constraints \textit{denials}. For example, a dead person cannot breathe:

\begin{lstlisting}
false :- person(X), dead(X), breathe(X).
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Denials are a special case of odd loops over negation~\cite{cacm-asp,gelfondkahl} and may be global or local. The denial above is an example of a global constraint. A local constraint will be imposed on specific predicates, and thus will be included in the body of the rule specifying that predicate. 

It should be noted that in ASP, the conjunction of \code{p} and \code{-p} is always assumed to be false. That is for every proposition or predicate \code{p}, the denial 

\begin{lstlisting}
:- p, -p.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent is asserted (the \code{false} in the head of a denial can be omitted). 

\section{Alethic Modal Logic in ASP}

First let us consider alethic modal logic. 
Alethic modal logic is the logic of necessary truths and related notions. Detailed exposition of alethic modal logic can be found elsewhere \cite{seph-deontic-logic}. Alethic modal logic considers six basic modal notions (with \code{p} denoting a proposition):

\begin{enumerate}
 \item it is necessary that \code{p}    
\item    it is possible that \code{p}       
\item    it is impossible that \code{p}       
\item    it is non-necessary that \code{p}      
\item    it is contingent that \code{p}     
\item    it is non-contingent that \code{p}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent It is well known that any of the first four operators above can be used to express the remaining five operators. If we denote the first one ``it is necessary that \code{p}'' as $\Box p$, then the remaining five can be expressed as 
% ART: What is diamond p below? 
%GG: diamond p means "possible p" (take a quick look at the Stanford encyclopedia entry for deontic logic
\begin{enumerate}\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item 
It is possible that $p$: $\Diamond p$ $\equiv$ $\neg\Box\neg p$
\item 
It is impossible that $p$: $\Box\neg p$
\item
It is not necessary that $p$: $\neg\Box p$
\item 
It is contingent that $p$: $\neg\Box p \wedge \neg\Box\neg p$
\item 
It is non-contingent that $p$: $\Box p \vee \Box\neg p$
\end{enumerate}


\smallskip
\noindent Alethic modal logic satisfies the basic axioms of modal logic shown below:

\begin{tabular}{l@{\hskip 2em}rl}
1. All tautologous formulas\\
2. \textbf{D:} $\Box p \rightarrow \neg\Box\neg p$ ($\Diamond p$)\\
3. \textbf{K:} $\Box (p \rightarrow q) \rightarrow (\Box p \rightarrow \Box q)$ \\
4. \textbf{NEC:} if $\vdash p$ then $\vdash \Box p$
\end{tabular}

\smallskip 
\noindent Alethic modal logic also satifies the following two axioms:

\indent\indent $\Box p \rightarrow p$ (if it is necessary that $p$, then $p$ is true). 

\indent\indent $\Box \neg p \rightarrow \neg p$ (if p is impossible, then p is false).


\noindent 
The second premise can also be written contrapositively as $p \rightarrow \Diamond p$, i.e., if $p$ holds then $p$ is possible. %
The six modal notions listed above are directly expressible using default negation and strong negation of answer set programming. 
ASP \cite{gelfondkahl,baral,cacm-asp} can model human thinking quite closely \cite{gelfondkahl,gupta-csr}, so we would expect that it can also be used to elegantly model modal logics. Note that aside from negation-as-failure, ASP also includes \textit{strong negation}. 
% ART: Why strong negation here and not classical negation? I feel that classical negation is more recognizable 
%GG: Strong negation is the preferred terminology in the community (keeps all the warring camps happy :-)
Negation-as-failure or default negation represented as \code{not p} evaluates to true if \code{p} is false or unknown. It evaluates to false if \code{p} is true. Strong negation, denoted \code{-p}, evaluates to false if \code{p} is true or unknown. It evaluates to true, if the falsehood of \code{p} can be explicitly established. Crucially, if \code{p} is unknown, then \code{-p} evaluates to false while \code{not p} evaluates to true. 

In our ASP representation of modal logic, negation appearing next to a proposition is treated as strong negation and that appearing next to a modal operator is treated as default negation or negation-as-failure. Thus, modal logic's $\neg\Box p$ translates to ASP's \code{not p}, while modal logic's $\neg p$ translates to \code{-p}. 
%
This mapping corresponds to the 3-fold partition of propositions in alethic modal logic shown in Figure \ref{threefold} taken from the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's article on deontic logic \cite{seph-deontic-logic}. Given a proposition, it is either true (necessary), false (impossible), or unknown (contingent). It is easy to see that default negation (\code{not p}) corresponds to ``impossible or contingent'', while default negation applied to a strongly negated goal (\code{not -p}), corresponds to ``necessary or contingent''. 

%It is easy to see that strong negation, which equates to impossibility of {\it p}, corresponds to the results ``possible'' and ``non-contingent'' in Figure \ref{threefold}, while negation-as-failure 
%\todo{non-necessary} corresponds to ``non-contingent'' and ``non-necessary''.

