This observation can be used to program various shades of truth and judgment calls through a combination of negation-as-failure and {strong negation}~\cite{gupta-csr} in ASP. For example, given a proposition \code{p}:
% (e.g., \code{p} $\equiv$ ``it is raining now''):
% ART: I think this example is not necessary because it is not brought up again.
%GG: we make use of it in making the connection with Alethic modal logic that comes right after. It makes it easier to also connect to Deontic logic (which is very similar to Alethic modal logic)


\begin{enumerate}
    \item \code{p}: denotes that \code{p} is \textit{unconditionally} true (necessary $p$).
    \item \code{not -p}: denotes that \code{p} \textit{maybe} true (possible $p$).
    \item \code{not p $\wedge $  not -p}: denotes that \code{p} is \textit{unknown}, i.e., there is no evidence of either \code{p} or \code{-p} (contingent $p$). 
    \item \code{not p}: denotes that \code{p} \textit{may} be false (no evidence that \code{p} is true, i.e., non-necessary $p$).
    \item \code{-p}: denotes that \code{p} is \textit{unconditionally} false (impossible $p$).
\end{enumerate}

\noindent As noted, the notion ``unconditionally true $p$'' maps to ``necessary $p$'', ``maybe true $p$'' maps to ``possible $p$'', ``unconditionally false $p$'' maps to ``impossible $p$'', ``unknown $p$'' maps to ``contingent $p$'', and ``maybe false $p$'' maps to ``not necessary $p$''. Thus, we can write these modal notions in the following way:

\medskip

\begin{tabular}{l@{\hskip 2em}rl}
 1. It is necessary that $p$: &$\Box p$ &$\ \equiv\ $ \code|p|\\
 2. It is possible that $p$: &$\Diamond p$  $\equiv$ $\neg\Box\neg p$ &$\ \equiv\ $ \code|not -p|\\
3. It is impossible that $p$: &$\Box\neg p$ &$\ \equiv\ $ \code|-p|\\
4. It is not necessary that $p$: &$\neg\Box p$ &$\ \equiv\ $ \code|not p|\\
5. It is contingent that $p$: &$\neg\Box p \wedge \neg\Box\neg p$ &$\ \equiv\ $ \code|not p $\wedge $ not -p|\\
6. It is non-contingent that $p$: &$\Box p \vee \Box\neg p$ &$\ \equiv\ $  \code|p $\vee$ -p|\\
\end{tabular}

\medskip

\noindent 
Notably, \code{p} and \code{-p} can never be true simultaneously. With this observation the modal square of opposition automatically follows (see Figure 2 in \cite{seph-deontic-logic}). The modal square of opposition states that if \code{p} is necessary, it is possible, and if \code{p} is impossible, then \code{p} is not necessary. In ASP, if \code{p} is true, then \code{-p} must be false, which implies that \code{not -p} (possible \code{p}) holds. Similarly, if \code{p} is impossible, then \code{-p} is true, so \code{p} must be false, and so \code{not p} (\code{p} is not necessary) must be true. 

% \begin{enumerate}
% \item
% It is necessary that $p$: $\Box p$ $\equiv$ \code{p}
% \item 
% It is possible that $p$: $\Diamond p$ $\equiv$ $\neg\Box\neg p$ $\equiv$ \code{not -p}
% \item 
% It is impossible that $p$: $\Box\neg p$ $\equiv$ \code{-p}
% \item
% It is not necessary that $p$: $\neg\Box p$ $\equiv$ \code{not p}
% \item 
% It is contingent that $p$: $\neg\Box p \wedge \neg\Box\neg p$ $\equiv$ \code{not p $\wedge $ not -p}
% \item 
% It is non-contingent that $p$: $\Box p \vee \Box\neg p$ $\equiv$  \code{p $\vee$ -p}
% \end{enumerate}


As stated earlier, strong negation captures the notion of impossibility, while negation-as-failure captures the notion of the modal not operator. 
The $\Box$ (necessity) operator disappears as we assume that if a predicate \code{p} is asserted then it is necessary. This assumption corresponds to the modal logic's necessitation rule mentioned above:
%
\begin{displaymath}
\vdash p \rightarrow \vdash \Box p
\end{displaymath}

\noindent That is, if \code{p} is in the answer set, it is necessary. With the mapping described above, formulas of alethic modal logic an be encoded in ASP and their satisfiability checked.  

\section{Deontic Modal Logic in ASP}

Next we show that deontic modal logic can also be modeled in ASP. Deontic logic is the logic of obligation, permission, and prohibition; and like alethic logic has six modal notions. 

\begin{enumerate}
 \item it is obligatory (OB) that \code{p} 
\item    it is permissible (PE) that \code{p}       
\item    it is impermissible (IM) that \code{p}       
\item    it is omissible (OM) that \code{p}      
\item    it is optional (OP) that \code{p}     
\item    it is non-optional (NO) that \code{p}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent The six deontic modal operators (OB, PE, IM, OM, OP, and NO) can be mapped one-to-one to alethic modal operators. We can express operators 2 through 6 using the OB$p$ operator:

\begin{enumerate}\setcounter{enumi}{1}
\item 
$p$ is permissible: $\textrm{PE}p \equiv \neg \textrm{OB}\neg p$
\item 
$p$ is impermissible: $\textrm{IM}p \equiv \textrm{OB}\neg p$
\item
$p$ is omissible: $\textrm{OM}p \equiv\neg\textrm{OB}p$
\item 
$p$ is optional: $\textrm{OP}p \equiv \neg\textrm{OB} p \wedge \neg\textrm{OB}\neg p$
\item 
$p$ is non-optional: $\textrm{NO}p\equiv\textrm{OB}p \vee \textrm{OB}\neg p$
\end{enumerate}

\noindent Given the parallel between deontic and alethic logic, obligation is referred to as deontic necessity \cite{seph-deontic-logic}. Standard Deontic Logic (SDL) is defined using the following axioms:

\begin{tabular}{l@{\hskip 2em}rl}
1. All tautologous formulas\\
2. \textbf{D:} $\textrm{OB}p \rightarrow \neg\textrm{OB}\neg p$ ($\textrm{PE}p$)\\
3. \textbf{K:} $\textrm{OB}(p \rightarrow q) \rightarrow (\textrm{OB} p \rightarrow \textrm{OB} q)$ \\
4. \textbf{NEC:} if $\vdash p$ then $\vdash \textrm{OB} p$
\end{tabular}

\noindent However, the deontic analogs of two principles for alethic necessity cited earlier are clearly false:

\indent\indent If OB$p$ then $p$ (if it is obligatory that $p$, then $p$
is true).

\indent\indent If IM$p$, then $\neg p$ (if $p$ is impermissible, then $p$ is false).

\noindent This is because obligations can be violated (e.g., we may not pay a parking fine even if we are obligated to do so) and impermissible things do happen (e.g., a car is driven on the wrong side of the road).
% ART: I think it would be good to include a brief laymen's example here to give the reader context for the proceeding sections.
% ART: Suggestion: "For example, when borrowing something one is obligated to return it in the same condition, but it may be permissible not to if something out of one's control happened."
We will assume that all deontic formulas used are written as normal logic programs. We assume that the stable model semantics \cite{gelfondkahl} is employed and that these normal logic programs can be executed using an answer set programming (ASP) systems, specifically the s(CASP) predicate ASP system \cite{scasp,gupta-csr}. The semantics of modal logics is given in terms of possible worlds, so modal logic theories can be elegantly represented and reasoned about in ASP. 

Under the standard semantics of SDL, if $\phi$ is a logic formula, then OB$\phi$ is true in a world $w$ if $\phi$ is true in all the worlds $v$ accessible from $w$. If $\phi$ is true in a world $v$, then by axiom NEC, OB$\phi$ is true in the world $v$. Which then means that 
$\phi$ is true, in turn, in the worlds accessible to $v$. The important thing to note is that OB$\phi$ and $\phi$ both hold in $v$. So, to model SDL theories in ASP, we really just need to model the world $v$ where all the formulas that were true in $w$ will hold along with the formulas implied by the modal operators. 
