%$\phi$ can refer to an arbitrary formula

\subsection{Obligations as Global Constraints}

Formalization of deontic logic has been difficult, and there have been many controversies. Most of these issues arise due to the difficulties in representing  obligations and impermissibilities. Quoting from Kowalski's and Satoh's paper~\cite{kow1}:

\begin{quote}
  ``Deontic logic is concerned with representing and reasoning about norms of behavior. However, many authors have denied ``the very possibility of the logic of norms and imperatives''~\cite{hilpinen-mcnamara}. Makinson~\cite{makinson}, in particular, states that ``there is a singular tension between the philosophy of norms and the formal work of deontic logicians. $\dots$ Declarative statements may bear truth-values, i.e., are capable of being true or false, whilst norms are items of another kind. They assign obligations, permissions, and prohibitions. They may be applied or not, respected or not $\dots$ But it makes no sense to describe norms as true or as false.'' However, Jørgensen \cite{jorgensen}, while acknowledging this distinction between norms and declarative sentences, noted that there are ``inferences in which one or both premises as well as the conclusion are imperative sentences, and yet the conclusion is just as inescapable as the conclusion of any syllogism containing sentences in the indicative mood only''. The resulting conundrum has come to be known as \textit{Jørgensen’s dilemma}.''\footnote{References in this quote have been renumbered to reflect the numbering scheme in this paper.} 
\end{quote}


\noindent The conundrum can be resolved by considering that norms impose a \textbf{constraint} on the accessible worlds. That is, if OB$p$ is true in a world $w$, then \code{p} must hold in all worlds $v$ accessible to $w$. In other words, $p$ must be a logical consequence of the theory that entails such a world $v$. Viewed as a constraint, the obligation OB$p$ simply states that in any possible world that is a model of the theory, \code{p} must hold. Similarly, IM$p$ is true in only those worlds where negation of \code{p} holds. That is, \code{-p} is a logical consequence of the theory that entails such a world. 

As discussed earlier, denials are an integral part of ASP. An obligations or an impossibility can be expressed through denials in ASP. If OB$p$ (resp. IM$p$) holds in world $w$, then \code{p} (resp. \code{-p}) holds in every world $v$ accessible to $w$. Thus, the obligation \code{p} (OB$p$) can be explicitly enforced by asserting:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not p.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent The above denial only admits accessible worlds in which \code{p} holds. Likewise, the impossibility of \code{p} can be explicitly enforced by asserting:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not -p.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Given a rule: 

\begin{lstlisting}
p :- q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent the obligation to enforce it OB($(q_1, q_2, \dots, q_n) \rightarrow p$) is expressed by asserting the denial: 

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not p, q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent This constraint will reject any possible world in which the conjunction of \code{not p, q$_1$, $\dots$, q$_n$} is not false. 
%
Similarly, given the rule: $q_1, q_2, \dots, q_n \rightarrow \textrm{OB}p$, 
then if \code{q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$} are in the answer set, then \code{p} must hold in that world as well. Such a rule, hence, also translates to the denial:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not p, q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Likewise, given the rule: $q_1, q_2, $\dots$, q_n \rightarrow \textrm{IM}p$, 
then if \code{q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$} are in the answer set, then \code{-p} must hold in that world as well. Such a rule, hence, also translates to a denial:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not -p, q$_1$, q$_2$, $\dots$, q$_n$
\end{lstlisting}



\subsection{ASP representation satisfies SDL Axioms }

Axioms D, K, and NEC are straightforwardly satisfied in our ASP representation. 

\begin{description}
\item[\textbf{D:}] Axiom D states that $\textrm{OB}p \rightarrow \neg\textrm{OB}\neg p$ is true. That is, $\neg\textrm{OB}p \vee \neg\textrm{OB}\neg p$ holds. Thus, $\neg(\textrm{OB}p \wedge\textrm{OB}\neg p)$ holds. In our ASP encoding this amounts to \code{p $\wedge$ -p} being false and, indeed, in ASP, for every proposition or predicate \code{p}, the denial \code{:- p, -p.} is always asserted. 
\item[\textbf{K:}] Given $\textrm{OB}(p \rightarrow q) \rightarrow (\textrm{OB} p \rightarrow \textrm{OB} q)$, the antecedent $\textrm{OB}(p \rightarrow q)$ translates in ASP to the denial \code{:- not q, p.} Looking at the consequent $\textrm{OB} p \rightarrow \textrm{OB} q$, $\textrm{OB} p$ translates to the denial \code{:- not p.} It is easy to see that any possible world in which the two denials hold, must necessarily have \code{q} hold, i.e., \textrm{OB}$q$ represented as the denial \code{:- not q.} holds.
\item[\textbf{NEC:}]  $p \rightarrow \textrm{OB}p$ trivially holds because if \code{p} is in an answer set, i.e., \code{p} is true in a possible world, then that possible world satisfies the denial \code{:- not p.}
\end{description}

\noindent It is important to note that denials in ASP can only perform checks, they cannot be used to draw new conclusions as rules with a non-empty head can do. Any world that does not have \code{p} inferred from other rules will not be allowed to exist if the denial \code{:- not p.} is applied. This essentially then precludes the axioms ``if OB$p$ then $p$'' and ``if IM$p$ then $\neg p$'' from holding. Recall that these two axioms hold only for alethic modal logic. 
%
This realization is crucial for resolving the ``conundrum'' mentioned above as well as resolving the various paradoxes that deontic logic suffers from.

%Note, additionally, that $p \rightarrow \textrm{PE}p$ will holds in our formalism. This is because $\textrm{PE}p$ translates to \code{not -p}, which is implied by \code{p}. However, its contrapositive $\textrm{OB}\neg p \rightarrow \neg p$ is translated into a constraint. One can perhaps conclude that permissibility and obligation are not strictly duals of each other.


\subsection{Preempting Obligations and Impermissibilities}

%There are, however, possible worlds where an obligation is violated or an imperissible thing happens. In such a case, the factors that cause the obligation to be violated or an impermissible thing to occur will preempt the corresponding constraint. For example, if the OB\code{p} constraint is preempted, then possible worlds will result where \code{p} does not hold. Likewise, if there is a violation of the IM\code{p} norm. The above can be readily modeled in ASP, as we show later. 


As noted earlier, obligations may be violated and impermissible
things do happen. In such a case, if a violation occurs, then the denial that represents that obligation must be preempted (dropped). Likewise, if an impermissible situation arises, then the denial representing the impermissibility must be dropped. This can be modeled in ASP quite elegantly because a global constraint can also be represented as an odd loop over negation (OLON) rule \cite{gelfondkahl}, i.e., a rule of the form:

\begin{lstlisting}
q :- B, not q.
\end{lstlisting}

The above rule gives a possible proof of \code{q}. 
Under the stable model semantics, if there is an alternative proof of \code{q}, i.e., a proof through other rules, then this OLON rule is taken out of consideration and does not contribute to the answer set. However, if there is no alternative proof for \code{q}, then \code{B} \textit{must} be false in every possible world (answer set).
%
This idea of representing obligations and impermissibilities as OLON rules can be used to elegantly resolve the paradoxes of deontic logic.
%
Given an obligation OB$p$, and a possible condition \code{c} that results in its violation, then instead of encoding the obligation as:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not p.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent we encode it as the OLON rule:

\begin{lstlisting}
c :- not p, not c.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent If \code{c} is false, then the above denial forces \code{not p} to be false, i.e., \code{p} to be true. If \code{c} holds (inferred through some other rules), then the above denial is ignored due to \code{not c} in its body. Thus, occurrence of a condition that violates an obligation results in that obligation being dropped (similar to what a human would do in such a situation).
%
Similarly, for an impermissibility, if an impermissible situation \code{q} (IM$q$) that can arise because of a condition \code{c}, we encode it as the OLON rule:

\begin{lstlisting}
c :- q, not c.
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Resolving Deontic Logic Paradoxes}

We show how various paradoxes of deontic logic  can be elegantly resolved by using ASP as the method of representation described above. Note that links to executable s(CASP) programs are provided for examples below in most cases. 

\subsection{Contrary-to-duty Paradox}
\label{ctdp}

Also known as the Chisholm's Paradox \cite{ronnedal2}, an example of the contrary-to-duty paradox is the following:

\begin{enumerate}
\item It ought to be that Jones goes to assist his neighbours.
\item 
It ought to be that, if Jones goes, then he tells them he is coming.
\item 
If Jones doesn't go, then he ought not tell them he is coming. 
\item Jones doesn't go.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent This information is represented in SDL as follows:

1. \textrm{OB}$go$

2. OB($go \rightarrow tell$)

3. $\neg go \rightarrow \textrm{OB}\neg tell$

4. $\neg go$

\noindent One can see that both OB$tell$ and OB$\neg tell$ can be inferred. We resolve this paradox by representing these rules in ASP as follows: \hfill \scasp{Chisolm1}

%\comm{To include/update links it is simple ---write the code in scasp playground, clink share to obtain the url and short it. Then, include a new reference in scasp\_links.tex following previous examples ;-).}

\begin{lstlisting}
-go :- not go, not -go.
:- go, not tell.
:- -go, not -tell.
-go.
\end{lstlisting}
%go :- not -go.
%:- go, not tell.
%:- not -tell, -go.
%tell :- go.
%-tell :- -go. 

%\noindent
%As we can see rules such as: {\tt tell :- go.} and {\tt -tell :- -go.} were not added. The deontic obligation of telling upon going is not saying that if Jones goes then he must tell, it is saying that in a world where Jones goes, he must also tell. Similarly for not telling upon not going. 

\noindent The OLON rule (line 1) says that Jones must go, unless \code{-go} is proved true through other rules. Line 2 says that in any possible world, \code{go} and \code{tell} must be true together. Similarly, line 3 says that \code{-go} and \code{-tell} must be true together. Note that lines 1, 2, and 3 above simply state the obligations as constraints. Whether Jones goes or not, and whether he tells or not, is completely independent of these constraints. 
So we can now imagine worlds where formulas in lines 1 through 3 are true, and we can choose whatever truth values we wish for \code{go} and \code{tell} by declaring appropriate facts. In the s(CASP) system, all the possibilities can be generated by declaring \code{go}, \code{-go}, \code{tell}, and \code{-tell} as abducibles. The resulting program can be loaded on s(CASP) and the various possible worlds obtained by issuing the appropriate queries. %The appendix shows the traces of these queries.

%Now if we consider lines 1-3 above, along with line 4, i.e., jones did not go, then the obligation to go in line 1 is ignored as there is alternative proof of Jones not going in line 4. The only possible world will be one with \code{-go} and \code{-tell}.

Given the program in lines 1-3 above, and the abducibles, we will find that the only admissible worlds are \code|{-tell,  not tell,  -go}| and \code|{tell,  not -tell,  go}|. In the former case, \code{-go} being true will preempt the constraint on line 1. Thus, the Chisholm's paradox is elegantly resolved.  
%: a contrary-to-duty obligation preempts compatible-with-duty obligation. 
%
%
For a clearer understanding, we can make preemption of the obligation explicit:

\begin{lstlisting}
ignore_obligation :- not go, not ignore_obligation.
:- go, not tell.
:- -go, not -tell.
ignore_obligation :- -go.
-go.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Note that it may appear that we can use an alternative simpler formulation where the OLON constraint in line 1 is re-written as the following, which states that Jones must go, if there is no evidence that he did not go:

\begin{lstlisting}
go :- not -go.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent However, this encoding suggests that if we don't have any information regarding whether Jones went or not (i.e., neither \code{go} or \code{-go} is known), but he decides to tell (i.e., we add \code{tell} as a fact), then our theory would infer that he did go, even though we are uncertain if he did go or not (e.g., despite telling, Jones may not have any intention to go). In other words, the following program will force us to infer \code{go}, even though Jones' acts of going and telling are completely independent. Essentially, an obligation or impermissibility should translate into a denial (global constraint), they should not be used for making inferences. \hfill \scasp{Chisolm02}

\begin{lstlisting}
go :- not -go.
:- go, not tell.
:- -go, not -tell.
tell.
\end{lstlisting}

The other similar paradox relating to strengthening the antecedent \cite{christian-strasser} are similarly resolved. Consider, for example:

1. Don’t eat with your fingers. 

2. If you are served asparagus, you must eat it with your fingers.

3. You are served asparagus.  

\noindent In modal logic this is represented as: \textrm{OB}fingers;  asparagus $\rightarrow$ \textrm{OB}fingers; asparagus. In our formalism, this will be encoded as: \hfill \scasp{asparagus}

\begin{lstlisting}
asparagus :- not -fingers, not asparagus.
:- asparagus, not fingers.
asparagus.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent With this encoding, if \code{asparagus} is served, \code{fingers} must be true. If \code{asparagus} is not served (e.g., the third line is replaced with \code{apple}), \code{-fingers} will be enforced. 

Many complex solutions have been suggested in the literature in the last 50 years to resolve the contrary-to-duty paradox, e.g., \cite{christian-strasser,ronnedal,prakken-sergot-dyadic} (see \cite{ronnedal2} for a survey of the approaches). These include making the `ought' context sensitive by differentiating between \textit{factual} and \textit{deontic} detachments \cite{seph-detachment}, using dynamic semantics to account for changes in obligations over time \cite{vaneck,castaneda}, using dyadic deontic logic \cite{hansson,prakken-sergot-dyadic}, and employing the concepts of sanctions, ordering of models, and abductive logic programming \cite{kow1}, etc. Since humans can deal with deontic paradoxes easily, their resolution in a logical framework should also be simple. The solutions suggested in the literature are quite complex, in our opinion (see for example \cite{christian-strasser}). We believe that complexities are significantly reduced by treating an obligation as a global constraint on the accessible worlds. 

One of the approaches to resolving the paradox is to use dynamic semantics to account for change in obligations over time \cite{castaneda}. There have been many attempts to try to solve Chisholm's paradox by carefully distinguishing the time points of the obligations \cite{thomason81b}. As discussed by Prakken and Sergot \cite{prakken-sergot}, there are versions of the paradox that cannot be solved by analyzing obligations over time, such as the following:

(1) It ought to be the case that there are no dogs.

(2) It ought to be the case that if there are no dogs, then there are no warning signs.

(3) If there are dogs, then it ought to be the case that there are warning signs.

(4) There are dogs.

\noindent using our framework, this is simply modeled as: \hfill \scasp{dog}

\begin{lstlisting}
dog :- not -dog, not dog.
:- -dog, not -warning_sign.
:- dog, not warning_sign.
dog.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent The query \code{?- warning_sign.} posed in s(CASP) will yield the possible world: \code|{dog,  warning_sign,  not -warning_sign}|. If we replace line (4) with \code{-dog.}, the possible world obtained is: %
\code|{-dog, -warning_sign,  not dog,  not warning_sign}|.

\subsection{Forrester's Paradox}   

Forrester's paradox is a variation of the contrary-to-duty paradox and is stated as follows:

1. It is forbidden for a person to kill, i.e., \textrm{OB}$\neg kill$

2. But if a person kills, he ought to kill gently, i.e., $kill \rightarrow \textrm{OB}kill\_gently$ 

3. If a person kills gently, then the person kills, i.e., $kill\_gently \rightarrow kill$

\noindent Suppose, that Smith kills Jones. Then he ought to kill him gently. But, by the axiom NEC, Smith ought to kill Jones, which contradicts the first obligation, that Smith ought not to kill Jones. These 3 statements can be encoded as: \hfill \scasp{kill}

\begin{lstlisting}
kill :- not -kill, not kill.
:- kill, not kill_gently.
kill :- kill_gently.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent With this encoding, in any possible world where \code{kill} is true, \code{kill_gently} will also be true. So it is not possible for Smith to kill, without killing gently. Additionally, the above theory admits possible worlds where \code{-kill} holds. 

\subsection{Sartre's Dilemma}

We resolve Sartre's dilemma in a similar manner. 

1. Join the French resistance. i.e. $\textrm{OB}join$

2. Stay at home and look after his aged mother. i.e. $\textrm{OB}stay$

3. 
Joining and staying are incompatible. i.e. $\neg(join \wedge stay)$

\noindent The ASP encoding is as follows. It states that staying preempts joining and vice versa. \hfill \scasp{Sartre}

\begin{lstlisting}
join :- not stay, not join.
stay :- not join, not stay.
:- stay, join.
\end{lstlisting}

If we assign all possible combination of truth values to \code{join} and \code{stay}, then there are only two worlds possible: \code|{join, not stay}| and \code|{stay, not join}|.
%
This paradox is similar to Kant's Law Puzzle \cite{seph-kant-puzzle}:

1. I'm obligated to pay you back \$10 tonight.

2. I can't pay you back \$10 tonight (e.g., just gambled away everything). 

\noindent encoded as: \hfill \scasp{Kant}

\begin{lstlisting}
broke :- not pay, not broke.
:- broke, pay. 
broke.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent There is implicit knowledge that if a person is broke, they cannot pay, which is encoded as a constraint. In any possible world entailed by these formulas, \code{broke} and \code{pay} cannot be true together. However, if \code{broke} is false, \code{pay} must be true. 
