rob :- rob, help.  % ...(3)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent First of all, the rule in line 3 will always produce \code{rob} as false under ASP semantics, since it's a co-recursive rule. Assume for the moment that we have co-inductive ASP \cite{coasp} where a rule such as \code{p :- p.} has two models: $\{$\code{p = true}$\}$ and $\{$\code{p = false}$\}$. In such a case, the possible world only exists because we \textit{assume} \code{rob} to be true. Additionally, obligation to \code{rob} does not exist in isolation, as obligation to \code{help} must also be there (along with the assumption that Jones has been robbed). So the Good Samaritan paradox is not really a paradox either. 

Note that the statement ``It ought to be the case that Jones helps Smith who has been robbed'' can be interpreted as ``If Smith is robbed, then Jones ought to help him'', which will be encoded as 

\begin{lstlisting}
:- rob, not help.
rob :- rob, help.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent The argument made earlier still applies, and again, arguably, the Good Samaritan paradox is not really a paradox. 

%Our aim is to show how normative statements can be expressed in Answer Set Programming. We use the s(CASP) goal-directed ASP system for illustration. 
%While we show how Deontic Logic can be realized in ASP, the implementation outlined can also be realized in systems such as XSB and ErgoAI that support well-founded semantics of negation since all the programs we will consider are stratified with respect to negation (but with integrity constraints explicitly allowed). Note that ASP supports stable model semantics that allows rules that are not stratified with respect to negation. 



\subsection{Modeling Impermissibility and Permission}

Thus far we focused on modeling obligations. Prohibition and permissibility can be modeled quite elegantly in ASP as well. A prohibition is a statement that disallows a (combination, i.e., conjunction of) condition(s) to be true. Prohibition can be represented in ASP as a denial (a global constraint). Finally, the notion of permissibility can be modeled via default rules (i.e., defeasible rules), where a certain predicate is true given that certain other conditions hold and there are no exceptions. For example, consider the following statements. A car must obey the speed limit. However, it is permissible for a police car to go faster than the speed limit as long as it is a safe speed. These statements can be modeled as: \hfill \scasp{speed}

\begin{lstlisting}
max_speed(X,Y) :- car(X), speed_limit(L), Y .>. 0,
                  Y .=<. L, not police_car(X). 
max_speed(X,Y) :- police_car(X), Y .>. 0,
                  safe_speed(Z), Y .=<. Z.
\end{lstlisting}

\section{A Comprehensive Example}

Consider a more complex example taken from \cite{seph-deontic-logic}. 
{\it John is obligated to return his friend’s car by noon, and the least he can do is return it on time with the same level of battery charge it had when he borrowed it. It is permissible to not maintain the same battery level if the car needs a battery change.}

This example makes use of ``least one can do'' modal operator \cite{seph-deontic-logic} that corresponds to a weak obligation. If a stronger obligation is not met, there is a weaker obligation or alternative action that must be fulfilled. ``Least one can do'' represents the minimum level of compliance. In this example, the stronger obligation is to return the car (whether or not in time or with a properly charged battery). This example also illustrates how secondary obligations can be modeled. 
%We, therefore, model the weaker obligation as a regular obligation that can be overridden. 
If the primary obligation (to return the car) is not met, then secondary obligations (return before noon, return with appropriate battery level) do not apply either. 

The representation in ASP code shown below is reasonably straightforward, given the discussion above. An obligation is modeled as a denial (constraint), and permissibility is modeled as a default rule. The code below is written using the s(CASP) goal-directed predicate ASP system that can also model continuous time through use of constraint logic programming over reals.
\medskip 

%\todo{use denials for integrity constraint to avoid ambiguity}. 

%possible actions:
%1: Car not returned
%2: Car returned, returned in time, with battery_ok
%3: Car returned, returned in time, with battery_not_ok
%4: Car returned, returned late, with battery_ok
%5: Car returned, returned late, with battery_not_ok
\noindent Encoding in s(CASP): \scasp{car03}
\noindent Encoding of a simplified propositional version: \scasp{car02}
\vspace{-0.1in}
\begin{lstlisting}
same_battery_level(C, T, T1) :- car(C), 
    batterylvl(C, T, L1), batterylvl(C, T1, L2), 
    diff(L1, L2, D), D #< 0$.$05*L1.

% modeling permissibility
battery_ok_to_return(C, T1, T1) :-
    same_battery_level(C, T1, T2),
    not abnormal_battery_status(C).
battery_ok_to_return(C, T1, T2) :- needBatteryChange(C). 
abnormal_battery_status(C) :- needBatteryChange(C).

%if a car is returned before noon, then the car was returned, 
:- returned_car_before_noon(J,X,C,Tr), not car_returned(J,X,C,Tr).

%if a car is being returned, it must have been borrowed earlier. 
:- car_returned(J,X,C,Tr), not borrowed_car(J,X,C,Tb), Tr #> Tb.

% Normally, only a friend will lend a car
% person J borrowed car C from person X at time T
borrowed_car(J, C, X, T) :- friend(J, X),
    own(X, C), abnormal_borrow(J, X).

% OLON rule to model the PRIMARY obligation to return car
fail_to_return_car(J, X, C, Tb, Tr) :- 
    not car_returned(J, X, C, Tr),
    borrowed_car(J, C, X, Tb), Tr .>. Tb,
    not fail_to_return_car(J, X, C, Tb, Tr).

% OLON rule to model the secondary obligation to return by noon
fail_to_return_by_noon(J, X, C, Tr) :- 
    not returned_car_before_noon(J, X, C, Tr),
    car_returned(J, X, C, Tr), 
    borrowed_car(J, X, C, Tb), Tr .>. Tb,
    fail_to_return_car(J, X, C, Tb, Tr),
    not fail_to_return_in_time(J, X, C, Tr).

% OLON rule to model the secondary obligation
%                         to return with battery level ok
fail_to_return_ok_battery(J, X, C, Tb, Tr) :- 
    not battery_ok_to_return(C, Tr, Tb), 
    car_returned(J, X, C, Tr), 
    borrowed_car(J, X, C, Tb), Tr .>. Tb,
    fail_to_return_car(J, X, C, Tb, Tr), 
    not fail_to_return_ok_battery(J, X, C, Tb, Tr).

fail_to_return_car(J, X, C, Tb, Tr) :- 
    borrowed_car(J, C, X, Tb), 
    wrecked(C, Tw), Tw .>.Tb.

fail_to_return_by_noon(J, X, C, Tr) :- 
    borrowed_car(J, C, X, Tb), 
    sick(J, Ts), Ts .>. Tb. 

fail_to_return_ok_battery(J, X, C, Tr) :- 
    borrowed_car(J, C, X, Tb), 
    financially_broke(J).
\end{lstlisting}

