\noindent Notice that the three obligations represented by the three OLON rules can be preempted by events such as car being wrecked (so it cannot be returned), the borrower becoming sick (so returns the car after the noon deadline), the borrower being financially broke (no money to charge the battery), etc. 
Note also that we have to have additional constraints to state that if a car is returned before noon, then that entails that the car itself was returned, and that if the car is being returned, it must have been borrowed earlier. 

\section{Related Work}

%Gopal: more needs to be added. 

There is a huge amount of work on resolving the paradoxes of deontic logic going back decades (e.g., \cite{old-deontic-paradox-paper}), however, to the best of our knowledge, none of the proposed approaches model deontic obligation and impermissibility as constraints that the accessible worlds must satisfy. 
Many researchers have also attempted to model deontic logic with ASP, however, unlike us, none map deontic modal operators to ASP's default and strong negation. In addition, most ASP-based approaches cannot handle predicates (only proposition), whereas in our case, deontic logic formulas can be mapped to arbitrary predicate answer set programs along with constraints over reals due to our use of the s(CASP) system. There are several works that use non-monotonic reasoning to attempt to resolve the paradoxes \cite{horty,kow1,kow2}. These efforts rely \textit{indirectly} on the connection between modal operators and default and strong negations. Kowalski and Satoh have proposed using abductive logic programming, which is closely related to ASP, for realizing deontic logic \cite{kow1,kow2}. In their approach, possible worlds can be ordered according to a preference relation. This preference relation is employed to select the correct world (model) in order to resolve these paradoxes. In our approach, in contrast, this preference is encoded in the logic of the program itself. The inappropriate worlds get ``killed'' in our method.

In other efforts, 
Governatori \cite{govern} has developed a domain specific language L4 for coding deontic logic which is then mapped to defeasible rules of ASP. In addition, an ASP meta-interpreter has also been developed for defeasible deontic logic. Our approach maps deontic logic directly to ASP. Cabalar et al \cite{cabalar-deontic} and Hatschka et al. \cite{eiter-deontic} also attempt to realize deontic logic with ASP. Hatschka et al. employ soft constraints to prioritize models (similar to \cite{kow1}). In their approach, various deontic modal operators are interpreted using ASP. Interestingly, they use only strong negation to model the negation of deontic logic. Default negation is not employed at all. In contrast, we map deontic modal operators directly to default negation and strong negation, and map deontic logic formulas directly to ASP code rather than interpret it with ASP.

Like Hatschka et al., Cabalar et al. also interpret deontic logic formulas (rather than map them into ASP rules like we do). They introduce predicates corresponding to modal operators, and additional formulas are defined to capture various constraints (e.g., p cannot be forbidden and obligatory at the same time). They use a weakened Axiom \textbf{D} called \textbf{wD}: $\neg$(O$p$ $\wedge$ F$p$ $\wedge$ not$~p$ $\wedge$ not $\neg p$). Deontic logic formulas are translated using the operators: O$p$, -O$p$, F$p$, -F$p$, not O$p$, not -O$p$, not F$p$, and not -F$p$, where O$p$ stands for $p$ being obligatory and F$p$ for $p$ being forbidden. Obviously, $p$ and $-p$ can also occur in the formula by themselves. Their encoding can result in an answer set containing both F$p$ and O$p$ due to their weakening of axiom \textbf{D}. Having both F$p$ and O$p$ in the same answer set is unusual. In contrast, our approach is simpler as it maps the F and O operators to ASP code and does not resort to weakening of Axiom \textbf{D} and so F$p$ and O$p$ will never belong to an answer set simultaneously in our approach. As a contrastive example, consider the following popular example taken verbatim from \cite{cabalar-deontic}:

(i) There must be no fence (f).

(ii) If there is a fence, it must be a white (w) fence.

(iii) If the cottage is by the sea (s), there must be a fence.

\noindent Cabalar et al's encoding, taken verbatim from \cite{cabalar-deontic}, is the following: 

\begin{lstlisting}
fb(f).                      % The fence is forbidden
ob(w) :- h(f), fb(f).        % CTD: if fence, it must be white
ob(f) :- h(s).               % Obligatory fence if by the sea
h(f).                       % We have a fence
h(f) :- h(w).                % White fence means fence
ob(f) :- ob(w).              % The same for obligation
:- ob(P), fb(P), not h(P), not -h(P).       % Weakened Axiom D
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent This program will produce the following model: \code{$\{$ob(w), h(f), fb(f), not fb(w), ob(f), not ob(s)$\}$}. \hfill \scasp{cabalar1} 

As we can see, both \code{fb(f)} and \code{ob(f)} are in the model, which appears counterintuitive. Also, the encoding is not completely correct, because if we remove the fact asserting that there is a fence (\code{h(f).}) and instead simply assert that the house is by the sea (\code{h(s).}), then the program will generate an answer set that does not contain a fence (i.e., it does not entail \code{h(f)}). The answer set does entail \code{ob(f)} and perhaps that is what was the intent of the authors was. However, if in addition to the fact \code{h(s).}, we also assert the fact \code{-h(f).} (there is no fence), then the above encoding still generates an answer set, even though one would expect \code{-h(f)} and \code{h(s)} to be incompatible (if the cottage is by the see, it must have a fence). The generated answer set is: $\{$\code{not ob(w), not f, not w, ob(f), h(s), fb(f), -h(f), not ob(s)}$\}$ which not only contains \code{ob(f)} and \code{fb(f)} that appear contradictory, but also \code{h(s)} and \code{-h(f)} which are contradictory. The paper's authors argue that the fence is both forbidden by the main obligation and mandatory by the sea-cottage rule, so a dilemma is produced, which could also be resolved by allowing a sea-cottage without a fence. We believe that such a model should not be admissible, since the whole idea is to override the obligation if there is a sea-cottage \cite{person-comm}. \hfill \scasp{cabalar2} 

\noindent Cabalar et al's encoding suffers from the problem that we mentioned in the beginning of Section \ref{ctdp}, namely, encoding an obligation to not go as 

\code{go :- not -go.}

%
\noindent In contrast, in our approach, the encoding will be as follows (h(X) is abbreviated to X): \hfill \scasp{GGencoding}

\begin{lstlisting}
f :- not -f, not f.  % Fence is forbidden (unless over-ridden)
:- f, not w.         % CTD: if fence, it must be white
f :- w.              % white fence means there is a fence.
:- s, not f.         % Obligatory fence, if by the sea.
f.                  % There is a fence
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent The possible world for our encoding is:
\code{$\{$f,  w,  not s,  not -f$\}$}. As we can see, our encoding is direct and simpler, and the answer does not contain contradictory information. 
%We could add a rule asserting that if there is a white fence, then there is a fence (\code{f :- w.}), but it is not necessary, and it won't change the results. 

Dyadic deontic logic has also been proposed  to handle the contrary-to-duty paradox by introducing a conditional operator that allows for obligations to be relative to a condition \cite{prakken-sergot-dyadic}.
This means that instead of just saying ``You ought to do A,'' we can say ``If B is the case, you ought to do A''.
This allows for a more nuanced representation of obligations, including those that arise as a consequence of violating other obligations. 
Our method can be thought of as taking the context into account as well to disable constraints that represent obligations in case these obligations are violated.  So, for instance, in our method, the obligation to go and help becomes inapplicable if Jones ends up not going.

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper we showed how modal logics can be implemented using the answer set programming technology. We showed how negation in modal logic maps to the two forms of negation---default and strong---present in ASP. We showed how the obligation and impermissibility operators of deontic modal logic elegantly map to global constraints (denials) of ASP. We also showed how contrary-to-duty and other paradoxes of deontic logic can be elegantly resolved using our approach. We also presented an extended example. Our work was initially motivated by our desire to automatically convert legal and policy documents into s(CASP) programs using Large Language Models. These legal and policy documents also contain normative sentences for which deontic modal logic is the most natural representation \cite{CODORD}. The various examples illustrate the directness and elegance of our encoding. 

