\subsection{Realizing Deontic Logic in ASP}

We have already seen how deontic modal operators map directly to default negation and strong negation in ASP. Next, we discuss how obligations can be modeled in ASP. As is well known, in ASP, inconsistencies and invariants can be modeled with denials (integrity constraints). An obligation is an invariant that \textit{must} hold. Thus, we will resort to ASP's denial construct to model obligations. 
%
The necessitation axiom of deontic logic states:

\begin{displaymath}
p \rightarrow \Box p
\end{displaymath}

\noindent That is, if $p$ is entailed by an answer set program $\Pi$, then $p$ is obligatory. So, for example, if $p$ is asserted as a fact in $\Pi$, then every answer set of $\Pi$ must contain $p$. We will exploit this notion to enforce the ``obligatory'' property.  Conversely, if $p$ is not entailed by our program, then those queries that entail $not~p$ may succeed. For example, if we have an answer set program not containing the fact $p$ (and there are no other rules for $p$), then this program will have answer sets that do not contain $p$. In such a case, if we wish to make {\it p} obligatory, i.e., only generate worlds in which {\it p} holds, then we must assert the global constraint

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not $p$.
\end{lstlisting}
\noindent This denial states that in every world that is possible, $p$ must be true. We rely on these ideas to simulate deontic logic with ASP.
%
As an example, consider the following rules:

\begin{lstlisting}
m.
r :- m.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Suppose we want \code{m} to be obligatory. Here, their is only one answer set, namely, \code|{m, r}|. Since \code{m} is a fact, it is present in every answer set of this program and the obligatory \code{m} property holds. Suppose now that we remove the fact \code{m.} Then, \code|{not m, not r}| is an answer set. That is, there is a possible world in which \code{m} does not hold. Clearly, \code{m} is not obligatory in such a case. To ensure that \code{m} is obligatory and no such world containing \code{not m} exists, we can assert the denial

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not m.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent which results in there being no answer sets for the program 

\begin{lstlisting}
r :- m.
:- not m.
\end{lstlisting}
% 

\noindent Given a general rule, 

\begin{lstlisting}
  p :- B.
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent where \code{B} is a (possibly empty) conjunction of positive and negative goals, and where we want to enforce $\Box p$, we can accomplish this, by asserting the constraint:

\begin{lstlisting}
:- not p, B.
\end{lstlisting}

This constraint ensures that every computed answer set that contains \code{B} also contains \code{p}. Note that if the body \code{B} contains a subgoal \code{q} that is obligatory ($\Box q$), that is, success of \code{p} depends on \code{q} being necessarily true, then in such a case, by the necessitation axiom ($q \rightarrow \Box q$) the enforcement of $\Box q$ boils down to enforcing \code{q}. In other words, the denial described above needs to be asserted for only those rules whose head is obligatory.  

%As mentioned earlier, this helps us deal with situation where $p$ is a derived predicate, meaning that the truth value of $p$ depends on the truth values of other predicates. The constraint above asserts that we will only consider those worlds in which $p$ holds true. 
