\section{\label{sec:examples} Model test problem}

During the sudden loss of confinement in a tokamak, the growth of a relativistic electron population called runaways often occurs with accompanying magnetohydrodynamic activity, as seen on machines such as JET and DIII-D. Runaway electrons (REs) and magnetohydrodynamic instabilities interact in a complicated manner, and how this interaction affects the final runaway current is not well-understood. 
A self-consistent magnetohydrodynamic and runaway electron model of a SPARC Disruption is used as the test problem for verification and performance studies in \S\ref{sec:perf}. In this scenario, runaway electron growth occurs in a plasma undergoing a sawtooth disruption, and the interactions between these two effects have been quantified in physics studies.
