\section{\label{sec:perf} Performance evaluation}

\textit{M3D-C1} evolves the velocity field according to (\ref{eq:L3}), where a Jacobian matrix is recomputed at every time step, without a non-linear solver iteration (eg, one Newton iteration only).
The setup for the production solver, block Jacobi (BJ), is primarily a full plane LU factorization on each plane.
The MG solver also requires a full plane LU factorization, but only one for the coarse grid.
This one solve is too large for a single process, as is common in ($3D$) multigrid, but is not nearly large enough to use the available resources effectively.
For these reason, the coarse grid is solved redundantly, which is faster and serves as a work-around for apparent MPI bugs in the solvers.
Four CPU nodes, or eight GPU nodes, are used for each redundant coarse grid solve (the nodes used for four planes).
Thus, the first case, the four plane case, does not use a redundant solve.
The MG solver also has a significant extra cost of the Galerkin coarse grid construction.
The solver setup is lagged with a constant \textit{refresh period}.
This results in degradation of the convergence rate before a solver refresh restores a consistent solver.
This degradation is more pronounced in the MG solver, which converges in just one iteration for most of these test cases.

\textbf{FieldSplit.} Performance experiments are restricted to the current production BJ solver and the MG solver with the PBJ smoother.
The \textit{FieldSplit} smoother is slightly slower and not investigated here, but \textit{FieldSplit} could be useful in the future with larger PBJ domains because vertex blocks of the matrix are $9x$ smaller than the monolithic smoother.

%
\textbf{Solver tolerance and verification.} The solver tolerance for each solver (BJ vs MG) is chosen so as to pass an \textit{M3D-C1} verification test where several quantities, $x$ (kinetic and magnetic energies, etc.), are compared with a converged solution, $x_0$ at each time step and deemed to pass if the \textit{relative} difference, $rtol=\frac{|x - x_0|}{|x_0|}$, is less than $10^{-3}$ for all quantities.
This verification test is relaxed for the growth rate (``gamma\_gr") at time step 41, for the smaller problems and steps 41-50 for the 64 plane case, because the sign flips between steps 40 and 42 for the smaller tests and flips twice in this region in the 64 plane case. The magnitude at step 41 is two orders smaller than that of steps 40 to 42, being near the zero of the function, and the 64 plane case has similar behavior.
The 64 plane case also has a sign flip of the growth rate at about time step 81 and the verification test was relaxed slightly.
The kinetic energies in 64 plane case triggered the original verification test in early time steps due to small values again: kinetic energies increase two to three orders of magnitude in the first 10 time steps or so.

\textbf{Solver parameters.} For the 4, 8 and 16 plane cases a relative residual tolerance of $rtol=10^{-6}$ was sufficient to pass this verification test for both solvers.
For the 32 and 64 plane case an $rtol=10^{-7}$ was found to be sufficient to pass a modified verification test, although the 64 plane case violated the test enough that an $rtol=10^{-8}$ would be reasonable.
All MG solvers use a $V(1,1)$-cycle, except the 64 plane case, which uses a $V(5,5)$-cycle, and the non-smooth iteration history in Figure \ref{fig:64_pl_mg} suggest that a $V(10,10)$-cycle would be a safer and perhaps faster choice.

100 time steps are run for all tests and the time step for the 64 plane case is one half that of the other tests, due to CFL constraints.
A solver refresh period of 20 is used for 4, 8 and 16 plane cases, and a period of 10 is used for the 32 plane case and the 64 plane case is refreshed every time step.
Note, these are not highly tuned and an optimal period is generally longer for the BJ solver.
%The 32 plane case uses a $V(1,1)$-cycle. % and the 64 plane case use a $V(10,10)$-cycle -- these two case are increasing difficult to solve, likely due to new nonlinear modes being resolved with more planes.
The MUMPS LU solver is used for all direct solves in this study.

\textbf{GPUs.}
%\subsection{\label{sec:gpus} Use of GPUs}
A significant driver in the algorithmic designs for iterative solvers in PETSC and for the solver developed in this work is the effective use of GPUs.
GPUs are very efficient in both time and energy if the application provides enough parallelism to allow many thousands of threads per GPU to work with only fast processor element (PE) synchronization, where a PE is a \textit{streaming multiprocessor} on NVIDIA hardware and a \textit{compute unit} on AMD hardware.
%The four plane test problem (Figures \ref{fig:4-MG}-\ref{fig:4-BJ}) is repeated with the use of GPUs in all numerics in Figure \ref{fig:gpu_times}.
The GPU nodes have one AMD socket on Perlmutter as opposed to two for the CPU nodes, which required the use of eight GPU nodes for a total of 32 NVIDIA A100 GPUs.
The 64 plane test case in this study is run with GPUs.
The solve phase, the time in the Krylov iterations after the setup, are similar to the CPU configuration performance, but the matrix triple product in the coarse grid constructor is about $5x$ faster on GPUs.
%The GPU runs use the same number of cores (MPI processes), 128, in both cases; thus, each GPU is driven by four MPI processes.
%Note, the solve and setup in time step 20 is less that the solve only in time step 19, indicating that this refresh period of 20 far larger than is optimal on GPUs.

