\section{\label{sec:future} Conclusion}

This work develops a toroidal semi-coarsening, geometric multigrid solver, with algebraic coarse grids and point block Jacobi (PBJ) smoothers in the PETSC solver framework, for \textit{M3D-C1}.
The new solver modestly sped up the largest test case, 64 plane, and dramatically sped up the easier, smaller, cases.
There are several optimizations to explore: the most obvious of which is adding $2D$ multigrid to the poloidal plane to develop a full $3D$ multigrid solver, and improving the Jacobi smoothers beyond vertex block subdomains is no less important.
More powerful, and expensive, smoothers will benefit harder problems like the 64 plane test herein, and $3D$ multigrid has the potential of reducing work and memory complexity of the solver substantially with little degradation of convergence rates.
%There are several improvements  but the primary need in this solver is the use of full $3D$ multigrid.
An effective full $3D$ MG would reduce the coarsest grid to a size where the cost of a direct solve is trivial.
Given the large anisotropies, high-order accurate FE, mixed zeroth, first and second derivative degrees of freedom, on complicated fourth order systems, these are challenging problems and the power of (unstructured) geometric MG in the poloidal plane is a likely candidate \cite{Adams-99a,adams2023bespokemultigridapproachmagnetohydrodynamics}. %, and algebraic multigrid may be be feasible.

The relatively simple solver presented here is demonstrated to be effective mathematically, especially when the preconditioner is refactored, refreshed, for each solve of these nonlinear equations.
However, this refresh cost is high.
These costs come from the coarse grid, full plane, direct factorization and the Galerkin coarse grid construction.
$3D$ multigrid will effectively eliminate the coarse grid factorization cost and greatly reduce the coarse grid operator construction costs.

The second primary area of improvement for MG solvers is expanding the options for the blocks in the point (small) block Jacobi smoothers.
The blocks in the current PBJ smoothers can be enlarged to some extent, so as to provide more powerful smoothers,  before the poor scaling of dense direct solvers kicks in.
The appeal of PBJ smoothers is the ability to use dense linear algebra (explicit inverses and BLAS2 inverse application), which performs exceptionally well on GPUs, but increasing the block size adds sparsity to these sub-solves.
Sparse batch solvers are a potential option for larger subdomains, but they tend to be simple as they must fit in kernel code on one PE \cite{adams2024performance}.
The choices for enlarging these blocks are line subdomains (eg, in the toroidal direction) and plane subdomains (eg, in the poloidal plane).
Furthermore, the solver domains, could be overlapped, to provide very powerful, though expensive, smoothers.
It is possible that different subdomains would be optimal for each of the three equations ($U, \omega, \chi$) -- in a \textit{FieldSplit} smoother -- because each of these equations represent distinct a wave in the system and could have different algebraic solver properties.

Some further comments and suggestions for future work are as follows.
\begin{itemize}

    \item $3D$ MG will greatly reduce the memory footprint of both the coarse grid factorization and of the coarse grids themselves, allowing for larger process subdomains, which will increase available parallelism and thereby increase throughput, especially on GPUs.

    \item The choice of using Galerkin coarse grid operators is driven by expediency, but using the \textit{M3D-C1} operator construction to generate the coarse grids would avoid the high memory complexity of the current matrix triple products (Kokkos, cusparse, hipsparse) available in PETSC, and could provide better quality coarse grid operators.

    \item The linear toroidal interpolation is of lower order than is recommended by multigrid theory for the cubic spaces in the toroidal direction. 
    Adding a term that couples the function and its derivative results in quadratic interpretation, ($\frac{L}{8}$, $L=2\pi R / np$, global radial coordinate of the vertex $R$ evaluating the derivative shape function at the midpoint). 
    This would be sufficient in theory and may increase the convergence rate with trivial extra cost. 

\end{itemize}

