\subsection{Finite Elements and Weak Form}

% C1Element.pdf

Galerkin method: integration by parts and shifting the derivatives from the unknown to the trial function
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\iint \nu_i \left[ \div{f(R,Z) \grad{\phi}} \right] dV = - \iint f(R,Z) \grad{\nu_i} \cdot \grad{\phi} dV
\\
\iint \nu_i \left[ \nabla^2 f(R,Z) \nabla^2 \phi \right] dV = \iint f(R,Z) \nabla^2 {\nu_i} \nabla^2 {\phi} dV
\end{array}
$$
$\nu_i$ is the $ith$ finite element basis function.

Mass matrix and high-order stiffness matrices are of the form %, 3D-Equation-2021.pdf, (1.8-1.9)

\begin{eqnarray}
	\begin{array}{lll}
\iint d^2R \nu_i \grad{\phi} \bullet \nabla_\perp \times R^2 &             & \iint d^2R R^2 \nabla_\perp \nu_i \times \grad{\phi} \bullet \\
\iint d^2R \nu_i R^2 \grad{\phi} \bullet                     & \rightarrow & \iint d^2R \nu_i R^2 \grad{\phi} \bullet \\
\iint d^2R \nu_i \nabla_\perp \bullet \ff{1}{R^2}            &             & -\iint d^2R \ff{1}{R^2} \nabla_\perp \nu_i \bullet
	\end{array}
\end{eqnarray}

$$
U(R,Z,t^{n+1}) = \sum_{i=1}^{18} U_i^n \nu_i(R,Z)
$$

\noindent
These 3 projections, together with the form of the velocity field, have the effect of approximately separating the dynamics associated with each of the three MHD waves into separate diagonal blocks.
\begin{eqnarray}
	\left[  \begin{array}{ccc}
	\left[ \cdot \right]_U & & \\
	& \left[ \cdot \right]_\omega &\\
	& & \left[ \cdot \right]_\chi
    \label{eq:3b3mat}
\end{array} \right]
\end{eqnarray}


