\subsection{Orderings}

Let R be a typical major radius, and a be a typical minor radius. Define the ordering small parameter $\epsilon=a/R$ . 
Gradients in the $(R,Z)$ plane are assumed ordered as: $\nabla_\perp \sim 1/a$, 
however in the toroidal direction we have: $\grad{\phi} \sim 1/R$. 
It then follows that we can order the quantities that appear in the magnetic field and pressure as:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 f \sim R \epsilon^4 \tilde{f}\\
\Psi \sim R^2 \epsilon^2 \tilde{\Psi} \\
p \sim \epsilon^2 \tilde{p} \\
F \sim R \\
\grad{F} \sim \epsilon \tilde{f} \\
\grad{R} \sim 1 \\
\end{array}
$$

Here $\tilde{f}$, $\tilde{\Psi}$, and $\tilde{p}$  are dimensionless quantities of the order of unity. 
This gives for the relative magnitude of the three terms in the magnetic field $\B$:

$$
\begin{array}{ccccc}
	\grad{\Psi} \times \grad{\phi} &:& \nabla_\perp f &:& F \grad{\phi} \\
\epsilon &:& \epsilon^3 &:& 1+\epsilon^2 \\
\end{array}
$$

and for the three terms in the current density

$$
\begin{array}{ccccc}
	\grad{F^\star} \times \grad{\phi} &:& R^{-2} \nabla_\perp \Psi^\prime &:& \Delta^\star \grad{\phi} \\
\epsilon^2 &:& \epsilon^2 &:& \epsilon \\
\end{array}
$$

This corresponds to the standard low-beta tokamak ordering. 
Applying this ordering to the energy terms gives the following relations 
between the diagonal terms and their corresponding kinetic energies:

$$
\begin{array}{lll}
	\ff{\delta W_{11}(U,U)}{R^2(U,U)} \sim \epsilon^2, &
	\ff{\delta W_{22}(\omega,\omega)}{R^2\omega^2} \sim \epsilon^2, &
	\ff{\delta W_{33}(\chi,\chi)}{R^{-4}(\chi,\chi)} \sim 1. \\
\end{array}
$$

This implies 
that a plasma motion that is seeking to minimize the perturbed energy 
must not have a large $\chi$  component in the velocity field. 
Also, we see that the off-diagonal terms satisfy the following ordering:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
\ff{ \delta W_{12}(U,\omega)      \delta W_{21}(\omega,U)      } 
   { \delta W_{11}(U,U) \delta W_{22}(\omega,\omega) }
     \sim \epsilon^2, \\
     \\
\ff{ \delta W_{13}(U,\chi)        \delta W_{31}(\chi,U)    } 
   { \delta W_{11}(U,U) \delta W_{33}(\chi,\chi) }
     \sim \epsilon^2,
\end{array}
$$

so that the partial energy matrix is diagonally dominant in this sense.



