\section{Introduction (Mon 7 Jul 2025)}

A real-valued random variable $X$ is called sub-Gaussian if its tail probabilities
decay at least as fast as those of a Gaussian distribution.
In high-dimensional statistics and probability, it is
important to characterise such tail decay quantitatively.  
Two useful quantities for this purpose are defined as follows.
The \emph{sub-Gaussian norm} of $X$ is defined by
\[
 \sgnorm{X}
 \ = \ \inf\left\{K \in (0,\infty) \colon \E e^{X^2/K^2} \le 2 \right\},
\]
and the \emph{sub-Gaussian parameter}
of $X$ such that $\E \abs{X} < \infty$ is defined by
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:SGParameter}
 \sig_X
 \ = \ \inf\left\{\sig \in [0,\infty) \colon
 \E e^{s (X - \E X)} \le e^{\sigma^2 s^2/2} \ \text{for all $s \in \R$} \right\}.
\end{equation}
It is well known that $\sgnorm{X} < \infty$
if and only if $\E \abs{X} < \infty$ and $\sig_X < \infty$,
and both of these equivalent conditions are commonly used as
defining properties of a sub-Gaussian random variable \cite{Boucheron_Lugosi_Massart_2013,Vershynin_2018, Wainwright_2019}.

Importantly, both $\sig_X$ and $\sgnorm{X}$ are norms that induce a Banach space structure
on the space of centered sub-Gaussian random variables.
A classical result states that these norms are equivalent, and that
there exist universal constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ such that
\[
 c_1 \sgnorm{X} \le \sig_X \le c_2 \sgnorm{X}
\]
for any centered random variable $X$.
Since the foundational contributions of Kahane \cite{Kahane_1960},
upper and lower bounds for these constants have been reported in various works.
Despite this, the sharp values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ do not appear to be widely known.

In this paper, we fill this gap by proving that the best possible such constants are
$c_1 \approx 0.61$ and $c_2 \approx 0.83$.
More precisely, we prove the following result.

\begin{theorem}
\label{the:Main}
The sub-Gaussian norm and the sub-Gaussian parameter of any centered random variable
are related by
\[
 \sqrt{3/8} \, \sgnorm{X} \le \sig_X \le \sqrt{\log 2} \, \sgnorm{X};
\]
and $\sqrt{3/8}$ and $\sqrt{\log 2}$ are the best possible universal
constants for the above inequalities.
\end{theorem}

The following examples confirm that the constants in Theorem~\ref{the:Main} are the best possible.

\begin{example}
\label{exa:Normal}
For a standard Gaussian random variable $X$, 
the moment generating function equals $\E e^{sX} = e^{s^2/2}$ for $s \in \R$.
Furthermore, the moment generating function of $X^2$ equals
$\E e^{sX^2} = (1-2s)^{-1/2}$ for $\abs{s} < \frac12$
and $\E e^{sX^2} = \infty$ otherwise.
From these identities we find that
$\sig_X = 1$
and
$\sgnorm{X} = \sqrt{8/3}$.
In this case the lower bound of Theorem~\ref{the:Main} holds as identity.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
For a Rademacher random variable $X$ such that $\pr(X=\pm 1) = \frac12$,
the moment generating function equals $\E e^{sX} = \cosh(s)$.
By comparing the coefficients of the Taylor series
$\cosh(s) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{s^{2k}}{(2k)!} $
and 
$e^{s^2/2} = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{s^{2k}}{2^k k!}$,
we find that $\sig_X = 1$.
Furthermore, because $\E e^{X^2/K^2} = e^{1/K^2}$,
we find that $\sgnorm{X} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\log 2}}$.
In this case the upper bound of Theorem~\ref{the:Main} holds as identity.
\end{example}

\subsection{Related work}

To the best of our knowledge, the
sharpest upper bound that has earlier appeared in the literature is
$\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \le (3/2)^{1/2} (\log 2)^{1/4} \approx 1.12$,
reported in Li \cite[Theorem 2]{Li_2024+}, and citing 
\cite[Lemma 2]{Lan_Nemirovski_Shapiro_2012} as a technical inspiration for the proof.
Our novel upper bound in Theorem~\ref{the:Main} improves this earlier bound to
$\sqrt{\log 2} \approx 0.83$.  More importantly, our upper bound has the sharpest possible
universal constant.
Weaker earlier lower bounds are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \le \sqrt{3} \approx 1.73$ in Rivasplata \cite[Theorem 3.1]{Rivasplata_2012},
also in van Handel \cite[Problem 3.1]{VanHandel_2016}
\item $\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \le 2$ in Boucheron, Lugosi, Massart \cite{Boucheron_Lugosi_Massart_2013}
and Zhang and Chen \cite[Proposition 3.1]{Zhang_Chen_2021}
\item $\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \le \sqrt{2e} \approx 2.33$ in \Chafai \cite[Lemma 1.2.2]{Chafai_etal_2012}. 
\item $\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \le \sqrt{8} \approx 2.83$ in Pollard \cite[Section 7.2]{Pollard_2024}
\end{itemize} 

The lower bound $\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \ge \sqrt{3/8} \approx 0.61$ is not new.
It can by proved by plugging $s=3/4$ into Buldygin and Kozachenko \cite[Lemma 1.6]{Buldygin_Kozachenko_2000},
or by tracking the constants inside the proof of Wainwright \cite[Theorem 2.6]{Wainwright_2019}.
This lower bound is also implicitly present in Li \cite[Theorem 1]{Li_2024+}.
In earlier works, apparently it has not been mentioned to be the best possible universal lower bound.
Earlier weaker lower bounds are:
\begin{itemize}
\item
$\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \ge \sqrt{1/6} \approx 0.41$ in Pollard \cite[Section 7.2]{Pollard_2024}, and implicitly in Rivasplata \cite[Theorem 3.1]{Rivasplata_2012}, also in van Handel \cite[Problem 3.1]{VanHandel_2016}
\item
$\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \ge \sqrt{1/8} \approx 0.35$ in Boucheron, Lugosi, Massart \cite{Boucheron_Lugosi_Massart_2013}
\item 
$\sig_X/\sgnorm{X} \ge \sqrt{(\log 2)/8} \approx 0.29$
in Zhang and Chen \cite[Proposition 3.1]{Zhang_Chen_2021}.
\end{itemize}

